WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
Proposition two changes how California saves for bad budget

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:07.360
years.

3
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.160
State revenue depends heavily on income taxes, including investment

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.900
gains, so a falling stock market can leave a

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.200
large hole in the budget.

6
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.420
The rainy-day fund is a reserve account, not a

7
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:21.880
new tax.

8
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.920
Current rules stop requiring additional deposits when that account

9
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.020
reaches ten percent of General Fund tax revenues.

10
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.640
Proposition two raises that threshold to twenty percent and

11
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.040
requires more saving in particularly strong investment-tax years.

12
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.540
It changes the rules over time; it does not

13
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.820
instantly double the money already saved.

14
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.040
Imagine a school manager planning through an economic downturn.

15
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.560
Think of a household that saves when overtime pay

16
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:52.160
is high.

17
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.840
If those hours disappear, savings can cover bills.

18
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.180
A state reserve serves a similar purpose, although public

19
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.000
budgets and legal rules are more complicated.

20
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.180
Public services need budgets that can weather weak years.

21
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.000
For illustration, ten percent of one hundred dollars is

22
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.140
ten dollars; twenty percent is twenty.

23
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.420
The higher threshold creates room for a larger required

24
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.540
cushion, not a requirement to put away twenty percent

25
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:21.800
every year.

26
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.960
The measure also extends required extra debt payments from

27
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.920
twenty thirty through twenty forty, and expands the debts

28
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.200
those payments can address.

29
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.960
These are extra payments beyond ordinary repayment schedules.

30
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.520
Deposits would generally count against the state spending limit

31
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.580
when withdrawn rather than when saved.

32
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.440
Emergency rules still allow deposits to stop or reserves

33
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.220
to be used.

34
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.780
The analyst expects higher reserves, without a fixed dollar

35
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:50.420
forecast.

36
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.640
A voter might prefer a stronger buffer against service

37
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.060
cuts, emergency borrowing, or tax increases during a downturn.

38
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.120
Paying debt earlier can also reduce future interest.

39
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.740
The appeal is more resilience when revenue is unpredictable.

40
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.080
You might ask what saving more leaves unfunded today.

41
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.340
Another voter could prioritize services or infrastructure now.

42
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.500
Saving more means less available for current uses, and

43
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.980
changing spending-limit treatment can affect when excess revenue must

44
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.100
be returned or allocated.

45
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.060
A larger reserve also cannot eliminate every recession shortfall.

46
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.720
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 60

47
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.120
percent said yes, 39 percent no, and 1 percent

48
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:37.620
were unsure.

49
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.800
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

50
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.660
Yes changes reserve and extra-debt-payment rules; no keeps the

51
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:48.360
current rules.

52
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.620
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

53
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.500
and official voter guide.

54
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.840
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
